# AbleSharp.Lib - Core Library Rules

## Purpose
AbleSharp.Lib is the core library that implements the Ableton Live Set format. It provides low-level data structures and serialization/deserialization for .als files.

## Architecture

### Project Structure
```
AbleSharp.Lib/
├── Automation/       # Automation envelopes, events, lanes
├── Clips/           # Audio/MIDI clips, notes, slots
├── Common/          # Shared classes and utilities
├── Device/          # Devices, parameters, modulation
├── FilesAndSamples/ # File references, samples, warp markers
├── Mixer/           # Mixer, sends, volumes, tempo
├── Project/         # AbletonProject, LiveSet, Scene
├── Routing/         # Audio/MIDI routing
├── Schema/          # Schema generation and conversion
├── Sequencer/       # Sequencer, recording, freeze
├── Tracks/          # Track types (Audio, MIDI, Group, etc.)
├── Transport/       # Transport, tempo automation
└── UI/              # UI state and view-related classes
```

## Coding Guidelines

### Class Design
- **Data Classes**: Most classes in this library are data containers matching the Ableton XML schema
- Use **auto-properties** for simple properties
- Use **init** accessors for immutable properties where appropriate
- Implement **INotifyPropertyChanged** only when needed for UI binding (GUI project)
- Keep business logic minimal - this is primarily a data layer

### Property Patterns
```csharp
// Simple property with backing field
public string? Name { get; set; }

// Property with validation
private int _tempo;
public int Tempo
{
    get => _tempo;
    set
    {
        if (value < 20 || value > 999)
            throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(value));
        _tempo = value;
    }
}

// Collection property - initialize to prevent nulls
public List<Track> Tracks { get; set; } = new();
```

### Schema and Serialization
- Classes should map to Ableton Live XML structure
- Use attributes for XML serialization when needed
- Property names should match XML element names (case-sensitive)
- Handle optional elements with nullable types
- Support multiple schema versions using `SchemaTypeResolver`

### Schema Generation
- `SchemaGenerator` class generates C# classes from schema files
- Generated classes go in `Schema/Generated/` directory
- Don't manually edit generated files
- Use partial classes to extend generated types if needed

### Value Objects
- Use `Value` class for wrapped primitive values (e.g., `Manual.Val`)
- Implement proper equality for value types
- Consider using records for immutable value objects

### Collections and Wrappers
- Use List wrapper classes (e.g., `TracksListWrapper`, `ClipSlotsListWrapper`)
- Wrappers provide type safety and encapsulation
- Implement proper collection initialization

```csharp
public class TracksListWrapper
{
    public List<Track> Tracks { get; set; } = new();
    
    public void Add(Track track) => Tracks.Add(track);
    public void Clear() => Tracks.Clear();
    public int Count => Tracks.Count;
}
```

### Enumerations
- Use enums for fixed sets of values (e.g., `MonitoringEnum`)
- Provide XML serialization support for enums
- Consider using `EnumEvent` for automation of enum parameters

### Event Classes
- `FloatEvent`: Floating-point automation events
- `BoolEvent`: Boolean automation events  
- `EnumEvent`: Enumeration automation events
- All events have `Time` and value properties
- Implement `IComparable` for time-based sorting

### Automation System
- `AutomationLane`: Container for automation data
- `AutomationEnvelope`: Defines automation target and events
- `ClipAutomation`: Automation within clips
- Support both arranger and clip automation

### Track System
- Base class: `Track`
- Derived types: `AudioTrack`, `MidiTrack`, `GroupTrack`, `ReturnTrack`, `MainTrack`
- Each track has `DeviceChain`, `Mixer`, routing
- Implement proper inheritance hierarchy

### Device Chain
- `DeviceChain` contains audio effects and instruments
- `Device` base class for all devices
- `Parameter` class for device parameters
- Support modulation targets and automation

### Project Structure
- `AbletonProject`: Root container
- `LiveSet`: Main content container
- `Scene`: Arranger scene/marker
- Maintain proper parent-child relationships

### File I/O
- Use `AbletonProjectHandler` for load/save operations
- Support gzip compression for .als files
- Validate XML structure on load
- Handle version migrations gracefully

### Testing Approach
- Unit tests go in same project (NUnit)
- Test serialization/deserialization round-trips
- Test version compatibility
- Mock I/O operations
- Test edge cases and error conditions

## Best Practices

### Nullability
- Use nullable reference types appropriately
- Mark optional properties as nullable
- Validate non-null requirements in constructors
- Use null-forgiving operator (`!`) only when certain

### Performance
- Lazy-load large data structures when possible
- Use efficient collection types (List<T> over ArrayList)
- Avoid unnecessary allocations
- Consider memory usage for large projects

### Versioning
- Support multiple Ableton versions (11.0 - 12.1)
- Use `MinorVersion` property to track project version
- Handle version-specific features gracefully
- Don't break backward compatibility

### Error Messages
- Provide context in exceptions
- Include file paths, line numbers when relevant
- Suggest remediation steps
- Use specific exception types

### Documentation
- Document XML element mappings
- Explain complex algorithms (e.g., note generation)
- Provide examples for non-obvious usage
- Document version-specific behavior

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid
- Don't add UI logic in the core library
- Don't add direct dependencies on third-party audio libraries
- Avoid tight coupling between subsystems
- Don't duplicate schema classes - use generated types
- Avoid mutable shared state
